  "text": "Yes. I’ll come as soon as everyone is safely ashore. I’ve been carrying this page for so long; I’d like to read it to her.\n\nHe raised the bird and looked underneath. Then he turned it toward me. “You can see where we fixed it.” “Yes,” I said.\n\nA boat is arriving. I need to help these passengers ashore. We can talk when they are safely off the boat.\n\nGrandma has agreed to start later. Let me know who will read my story when you have made the plan.\n\nI let them pass. The boy lowered his flute and looked toward the back of the boat once more.\n\nI’m still helping passengers. I’ll stay at the dock while you share stories in the garden.\n\nPlease ask her. Starting after the last boat returns would give me time to finish work.\n\nThank you. If I’m still at work, I’d love to read one of Grandma’s stories afterward.\n\nTake this copy with you. Tell Grandma I still want to hear her stories, too.\n\nI’m still helping passengers here. I’d like to hear how the gathering went.\n\nThank you for telling Grandma about my work. Let me know what you decide.\n\n“One I wrote for Grandma’s garden. I keep bringing it to work with me.”\n\nI’m glad we found a time when I could join everyone in the garden.\n\nSo have I. I used to arrive too late even to hear how they ended.\n\nThank you for bringing Grandma’s story. I’ll read it after work.\n\nYou are speaking as Mara. Choose your response.\n\n“But I would like Grandma to hear my story.”",
